WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.160
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.160
A telescope does not bring the Moon physically closer.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:07.560
It makes the Moon occupy a larger angle at your eye.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.200
That is our key idea for optical instruments: angular magnification.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:18.000
A small object of height h at distance d subtends an angle approximately h

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.920
divided by d, when that angle is small and expressed in radians.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.900
Bring the object closer and its angular size increases, but your eye cannot

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.320
focus clearly at arbitrarily small distances.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.480
For standard JEE calculations, we take the least distance of distinct vision,

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.480
D, as twenty five centimeters unless another value is given.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.020
It is a conventional reference, not a promise that every person's eye has

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.140
exactly that near point.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:49.320
A one millimeter object viewed at twenty five centimeters subtends about zero

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:50.940
point zero zero four radians.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.880
A magnifier lets us see that object under a larger angle while keeping it in

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:56.140
focus.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.420
Angular magnification compares the angle with the instrument against the

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.040
specified unaided viewing angle.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.300
Image height magnification, such as v over u for a lens, answers a different

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:07.680
question.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.780
Mixing those two definitions causes trouble in microscope questions.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.840
Throughout this class we use ideal paraxial instruments, ignore aberrations,

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.340
and specify where the final image lies.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:24.048
The final image at infinity corresponds to a relaxed eye in the standard

00:01:24.048 --> 00:01:24.400
model.

00:01:24.648 --> 00:01:28.608
A simple magnifier is a converging lens viewed close to the eye.

00:01:29.368 --> 00:01:33.328
For a final virtual image at infinity, place the object at the lens's focal

00:01:33.328 --> 00:01:33.608
plane.

00:01:34.408 --> 00:01:38.620
The outgoing rays are parallel, and the angle they make is approximately h

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:38.848
over

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:39.148
f.

00:01:39.948 --> 00:01:44.608
Divide by the unaided reference angle h over D, and angular magnification is D

00:01:44.608 --> 00:01:45.148
over f.

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:50.548
A lens of focal length five centimeters gives magnification five when D is

00:01:50.548 --> 00:01:51.508
twenty five centimeters.

00:01:51.508 --> 00:01:56.848
Now bring the object slightly inside the focus and arrange the final virtual

00:01:56.848 --> 00:01:57.868
image at the near point.

00:01:58.568 --> 00:02:03.308
With the lens close to the eye, the magnitude becomes one plus D over f, which

00:02:03.308 --> 00:02:04.608
is six for the same lens.

00:02:05.288 --> 00:02:07.808
The extra one belongs to this different viewing arrangement.

00:02:08.468 --> 00:02:11.448
It is not a correction you add to every magnifying power formula.

00:02:12.228 --> 00:02:13.728
Let's connect this to the lens equation.

00:02:14.508 --> 00:02:18.388
Set final image distance to negative twenty five centimeters and focal length

00:02:18.388 --> 00:02:19.348
to positive five.

00:02:19.348 --> 00:02:24.108
The object distance becomes negative twenty five over six centimeters, about

00:02:24.108 --> 00:02:26.008
negative four point one seven.

00:02:26.008 --> 00:02:29.068
That lies inside the five centimeter focus, as expected.

00:02:29.788 --> 00:02:33.548
The near point arrangement can give greater angular magnification, but the

00:02:33.548 --> 00:02:36.788
relaxed eye arrangement is often more comfortable in the elementary model.

00:02:37.488 --> 00:02:42.108
In a numerical problem, underline whether the final image is at infinity or at

00:02:42.108 --> 00:02:43.448
D before choosing the formula.

00:02:43.656 --> 00:02:49.196
A compound microscope uses an objective lens close to the small object and an

00:02:49.196 --> 00:02:50.496
eyepiece close to the eye.

00:02:51.096 --> 00:02:54.796
The objective makes a real, inverted, enlarged intermediate image.

00:02:55.396 --> 00:02:59.876
The eyepiece treats that image as its object and magnifies its angular size.

00:03:00.576 --> 00:03:04.856
Therefore total magnification is the objective's signed linear magnification

00:03:04.856 --> 00:03:08.016
multiplied by the eyepiece's angular magnification.

00:03:08.756 --> 00:03:11.436
Let's calculate one exact objective stage.

00:03:11.816 --> 00:03:16.196
Its focal length is two centimeters, and the object distance is negative two

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:17.016
point two centimeters.

00:03:17.876 --> 00:03:21.896
The lens equation gives image distance positive twenty two centimeters, so

00:03:21.896 --> 00:03:24.076
objective magnification is negative ten.

00:03:24.956 --> 00:03:29.156
With an eyepiece focal length five centimeters and final image at infinity,

00:03:29.816 --> 00:03:32.776
eyepiece magnification is twenty five over five, or five.

00:03:33.436 --> 00:03:35.396
Total magnification is negative fifty.

00:03:36.016 --> 00:03:40.536
Its magnitude is fifty, and the minus sign describes inversion relative to the

00:03:40.536 --> 00:03:41.316
original object.

00:03:42.256 --> 00:03:46.176
The intermediate image must lie at the eyepiece focal plane, so the lens

00:03:46.176 --> 00:03:48.276
separation here is twenty seven centimeters.

00:03:49.416 --> 00:03:53.696
In a high magnification approximation, objective magnification magnitude is

00:03:53.696 --> 00:03:57.216
about optical tube length L divided by objective focal length.

00:03:57.856 --> 00:04:01.636
L is the distance from the objective's back focal plane to the intermediate

00:04:01.636 --> 00:04:05.076
image plane, not blindly every quoted mechanical tube length.

00:04:05.916 --> 00:04:10.636
That gives the common approximate normal magnification L D over f o f e.

00:04:10.636 --> 00:04:15.696
For a near point final image, the eyepiece factor becomes one plus D over f e,

00:04:15.696 --> 00:04:17.636
and the geometry must be adjusted accordingly.

00:04:18.168 --> 00:04:22.308
For an astronomical telescope, the object is extremely far away.

00:04:23.268 --> 00:04:26.088
Rays arriving from one point of that object are nearly parallel.

00:04:26.948 --> 00:04:29.628
The objective lens makes a real image in its focal plane.

00:04:30.548 --> 00:04:34.952
For normal adjustment, place the eyepiece so this intermediate image is also

00:04:34.952 --> 00:04:35.068
at

00:04:35.068 --> 00:04:36.808
the eyepiece's incoming focal plane.

00:04:37.508 --> 00:04:41.248
The final rays are parallel, allowing the standard relaxed eye view.

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:46.308
The objective focal length is usually large, and the eyepiece focal length is

00:04:46.308 --> 00:04:46.548
small.

00:04:47.268 --> 00:04:51.168
Angular magnification is negative f o divided by f e.

00:04:51.168 --> 00:04:53.768
The negative sign means the astronomical image is inverted.

00:04:54.748 --> 00:04:58.248
If the objective focal length is one hundred centimeters and the eyepiece

00:04:58.248 --> 00:04:58.488
focal

00:04:58.488 --> 00:05:02.788
length is five centimeters, the magnification is negative twenty.

00:05:02.788 --> 00:05:07.493
The magnitude is twenty, and the lens separation is one hundred five

00:05:07.493 --> 00:05:08.008
centimeters.

00:05:08.008 --> 00:05:09.888
Why does that focal length ratio appear?

00:05:10.148 --> 00:05:14.888
A distant object angle produces intermediate image height approximately f o

00:05:14.888 --> 00:05:16.028
times the incoming angle.

00:05:16.548 --> 00:05:20.888
The eyepiece converts that height into an outgoing angle approximately height

00:05:20.888 --> 00:05:22.748
divided by f e.

00:05:22.748 --> 00:05:25.368
Their ratio is f o over f e.

00:05:25.368 --> 00:05:28.528
A telescope objective with a large aperture also collects more light.

00:05:29.488 --> 00:05:32.928
Magnifying power and light collection are different properties; a huge

00:05:32.928 --> 00:05:35.648
magnification does not automatically reveal more detail.

00:05:36.568 --> 00:05:40.368
These formulas describe an ideal astronomical refractor in normal adjustment,

00:05:41.128 --> 00:05:44.488
not an upright terrestrial telescope with additional erecting elements.

00:05:44.736 --> 00:05:49.036
A reflecting telescope uses a concave primary mirror instead of a large

00:05:49.036 --> 00:05:49.816
objective lens.

00:05:50.656 --> 00:05:54.496
Incoming rays from a distant object reflect toward the primary's focal region,

00:05:55.016 --> 00:05:56.836
where a real intermediate image forms.

00:05:57.436 --> 00:05:59.096
An eyepiece magnifies that image.

00:05:59.716 --> 00:06:03.636
A secondary mirror may redirect the beam to a convenient viewing position,

00:06:04.116 --> 00:06:04.976
depending on the design.

00:06:05.736 --> 00:06:10.416
Our board shows the primary image eyepiece sequence schematically; it is not a

00:06:10.416 --> 00:06:12.216
construction drawing for a particular telescope.

00:06:12.216 --> 00:06:17.516
In an ideal normal arrangement, magnifying power magnitude is approximately

00:06:17.516 --> 00:06:20.696
primary focal length divided by eyepiece focal length.

00:06:21.296 --> 00:06:24.896
A two hundred centimeter primary with a four centimeter eyepiece gives

00:06:24.896 --> 00:06:25.896
magnitude fifty.

00:06:26.676 --> 00:06:29.756
Reflection does not disperse different wavelengths through an objective lens,

00:06:30.256 --> 00:06:33.656
so the primary avoids the refractive objective's chromatic aberration.

00:06:34.236 --> 00:06:38.736
That does not mean every reflector is perfectly sharp: spherical aberration,

00:06:39.596 --> 00:06:43.116
alignment errors, diffraction, and atmospheric conditions can still matter.

00:06:44.176 --> 00:06:48.136
Telescope tube length also depends on the folded optical layout, so do not

00:06:48.136 --> 00:06:51.996
reuse the refractor's simple lens separation expression as the physical length

00:06:51.996 --> 00:06:52.856
of every reflector.

00:06:53.696 --> 00:06:58.357
Our final comparison is useful for JEE: a microscope begins with a nearby

00:06:58.357 --> 00:06:58.676
small

00:06:58.676 --> 00:07:01.896
object and uses a short focal length objective to enlarge it.

00:07:02.356 --> 00:07:06.756
An astronomical telescope begins with a distant angular object and commonly

00:07:06.756 --> 00:07:09.156
uses a long focal length objective or primary.

00:07:09.996 --> 00:07:14.021
Both then use an eyepiece, and both require the final image condition to

00:07:14.021 --> 00:07:14.356
choose

00:07:14.356 --> 00:07:16.076
the correct magnifying power formula.
